= February 2024 — A qualification with a price list

Prosecutors detain the rector of a Warsaw private business school and begin
examining several thousand MBA diplomas. The document matters because of what
it unlocks: an MBA exempts its holder from the state examination otherwise
required to sit on the supervisory board of a state-controlled company. Those
seats carry fees, information and patronage, and they are distributed by
ministers.

The list of people who took the short course is not a list of one party. It
contains officials, local politicians, company directors, uniformed officers
and parliamentarians from several camps, including people who have spent years
denouncing exactly this arrangement.

[? if left_in_government = 1: Some of the appointments now under examination
were made by ministries this coalition inherited, and a few by ministries it
now runs. An audit we order will reach our own nominees.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We can demand the audit without owning any of the
appointments, which is a real advantage and a very short-lived one.?]

[? if state_company_politicisation >= 12: We have already spent a campaign
arguing that state companies are run to a political calendar. This is the
staffing half of the same machine.?]
[? if left_dominant_current = "barons": Our own organisation has spent thirty
years learning how supervisory-board seats are distributed, and everybody in
the room knows it.?][? if left_dominant_current = "razem": Razem's people want
the whole exemption abolished rather than policed.?]

- @collegium_audit
- @collegium_abolish
- @collegium_partisan
- @collegium_chamber

@collegium_audit
title: Demand a full audit of every board appointment made on this exemption
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Reaches appointments made by every party, including any of ours.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot staff an audit demand it would also be answering.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; credential_patronage_exposure += 10; institutional_trust += 5; administrative_reform_pressure += 4; public_trust += 3; ko_relation -= 3; psl_relation -= 4; pis_relation -= 4; barons_dissent += 5; media_capacity += 2; news_headline = "The Left asks for every exempted board appointment to be published"

We ask for one list: every person seated on a state-company board under the
diploma exemption, the ministry that nominated them, the date and the fee. No
accusation, no names read out in the chamber, just the list — which is why the
demand is impossible to refuse in public and deeply unwelcome in private.

Several of the names will be embarrassing to people we need. At least one is
likely to be ours. The audit is worth having anyway, because a party that only
audits other parties' patronage is not auditing patronage.

- @poland_event_queue: Send the audit demand to the desk

@collegium_abolish
title: Legislate the exemption away and restore the state examination
subtitle: Removes the mechanism rather than the individuals; slower, and it protects everyone already seated.
on-arrival: administrative_reform_pressure += 6; administrative_capacity += 2 if left_in_government = 1; institutional_trust += 4; state_company_governance_pressure += 8; public_trust += 2; credential_patronage_exposure -= 6; news_headline = "A bill removes the diploma shortcut into state-company boards"

The bill is two pages: the exemption goes, the examination returns, and
sitting members have twelve months to pass it or leave. It fixes the door
rather than arguing about who walked through it, and it can be supported by
parties whose own people are on the list precisely because it is not
retrospective.

That is also the criticism. Nobody currently drawing a board fee for a
purchased credential is removed by us; they are removed by an examination
they may yet pass, or by an ordinary resignation in a year nobody will be
watching.

- @poland_event_queue: Table the exemption bill

@collegium_partisan
title: Read the government camp's names into the parliamentary record
subtitle: Maximum immediate damage to the largest holder of these seats; the scandal returns with our own names attached.
on-arrival: credential_patronage_exposure += 14; pis_relation -= 10; ko_relation -= 6; public_trust += 1; left_poll_momentum += 0.2; institutional_trust -= 3; party_finance_scandal += 4; media_capacity += 3; news_headline = "The diploma list is read out as a party-political indictment"

The names are read out, the fees beside them, and the clips run for a week.
The party that held the ministries longest supplied the most names, and saying
so is neither unfair nor untrue.

It converts a procurement-and-patronage scandal into a partisan exchange, and
partisan exchanges are settled by whoever has the next list. Ours is being
compiled now, by people who were on ours.

- @poland_event_queue: Read the list into the record

@collegium_chamber
title: Have the audit chamber trace the exemption through every state company
subtitle: Requires an open referral channel. Produces the appointment map instead of the arrest list.
choose-if: nik_referral_channel = 1
unavailable-subtitle: We have no channel into the Supreme Audit Office, and the ministries will not hand a rival caucus their own nomination files.
on-arrival: credential_patronage_exposure += 18; state_company_governance_pressure += 10; nik_government_hostility += 6; institutional_trust += 6; administrative_reform_pressure += 4; public_trust += 3; ko_relation -= 4; psl_relation -= 4; pis_relation -= 5; news_headline = "Auditors map every board seat filled through the diploma exemption"

The chamber does what prosecutors cannot: it walks the exemption through every
company in which the Treasury holds a stake, and produces the map — ministry,
nominee, date, fee, credential.

It is the least dramatic and most damaging version of this scandal, because it
is a document rather than an accusation, it names people in more than one camp,
and it is still being cited when the next government fills the same seats.

- @poland_event_queue: Commission the appointment map

